{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/docset at d77044c4ac9256cc488b06ee4fe737ab52fcb210. The no-workroom skip is pinned, and made immune to an inherited Git environment.\n\nWHAT IS HERE. One new file, internal/docset/no_workroom_test.go, and nothing else. It asserts that the docset gate skips explicitly, carrying the exact no-workroom reason, when run in a checkout that holds no workroom — rather than failing, or passing while measuring nothing.\n\nTwo things make it a real test rather than a shape. The gate has two different skips, and the reason string is what tells them apart, so the test matches the reason through a named constant instead of accepting any skip. And withoutGitEnvironment strips GIT_DIR, GIT_WORK_TREE and GIT_COMMON_DIR before the subprocess runs, so an ambient Git environment cannot redirect it at a real repository.\n\nEVIDENCE THAT BOTH OF THOSE ARE LOAD-BEARING, not assumed. I replaced command.Env = withoutGitEnvironment(os.Environ()) with plain os.Environ() and ran the test with GIT_DIR and GIT_WORK_TREE pointed at this repository. It failed, and the failure text is the informative part: the subprocess resolved the real workroom and took the genesis-mismatch skip instead, reporting that the set names workroom 1111... while the checkout holds 5d262274. So the stripping is what keeps the test in the no-workroom case, and the reason matching is what refuses the wrong skip. Without the constant, that mutation would have passed silently on the other skip. The mutation was reverted and the worktree verified clean; the head is unchanged.\n\nWHY THIS COMMIT EXISTS. Codex approved the substance at head 7d540d27 and requested changes for one reason: that head predated merge 27603774, so at its own exact commit five documentation pages still cited the retired f6c96085 and the docset gate failed. The content here is byte-identical to what was reviewed, verified with diff against 7d540d27; only the base moved. No logic changed, because none was asked to.\n\nThe error behind that rejection was mine and worth recording. I told the reviewer the head merged cleanly against a probe of main with the repair applied, measured with git merge-tree. That was true and it was the wrong property. Merge-cleanliness says a change will combine without conflict; it says nothing about whether the gates pass at the exact commit under review, which is what naming an exact head is for. A branch cut before a gate-affecting merge is stale for review even when it merges perfectly.\n\nGATES at this exact head, uncached: internal/docset 107.641s, go vet clean, go build clean, git diff --check clean, git merge-tree against main 27603774 clean.\n\nThis rests on 596cd629, the live non-stale predecessor at this exact path, whose commit 0f215b43 is an ancestor of main. The two candidates at abandoned commits, 6b3237a9 at a2e3f187 and d6eb003b at 7d540d27, are retired against this record.","body":{"commit":"d77044c4ac9256cc488b06ee4fe737ab52fcb210","path":"internal/docset"}}